<header class="main-header">
  <div class="series-title">Likutay Halachos · Choshen Mishpat</div>
  <h1>Hilchos Uraiv 5 — Part 1</h1>
  <div class="subtitle">Laws of a Guarantor — 5 (§§23–29) · Based on "Teekoo" — Likutay Moharan II:1 · The guarantor, the roots of arvus, and the discharge of debts through t'filah</div>
</header>

<p class="source" style="text-align:center; margin-bottom: 30px;">Based on the Torah "Teekoo" 1 in Likutay Tinyana, Siman 1. See there the entire Torah. Continuing from the previous sections.</p>

<div class="section-header">§23</div>
<p>And this is the aspect of what our Sages, <em>zichronam livrachah</em>, said: "Distance yourself from guaranteeing" <span class="source">(Yevamos 109b)</span>. For it has already been explained above (in §3) that all loans are drawn from having blemished the <span class="key-term">t'filah</span> — that one did not pray with proper <span class="key-term">kavanah</span> [intention] and did not connect his prayer to the true <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span>, who is the aspect of the great Lender. For all the worlds and all the celestial spheres and the sun and the moon and the stars etc. — all the more so this entire lowly world, the earth and all that is upon it etc. — are all in the aspect of borrowers who receive from him. And the entire discharge of their debt and loan depends upon man, for whose sake everything was created — who must pray with <span class="key-term">kavanah</span> and connect his prayer to the true <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span> who is the great Lender. Through this, all the powers return to him — which is the aspect of the discharge of all the aforementioned loans, as is explained above at length. See there.</p>
<p>And one who blemishes this — through this he falls into loans and debts etc. And because of this it is difficult to discharge the debts of the loans. For the essential discharge of debts and loans as they should be is through the perfection of the rectification of <span class="key-term">t'filah</span> — through which is the essential discharge of the loans etc., as above.</p>
<p>And for this reason one must distance himself from being a guarantor, as our Sages, <em>zichronam livrachah</em>, said. For who can insert himself into this — to discharge the debts and loans of his fellow? For it is enough if he can extricate himself to discharge the debts of his own loan — that is, that he should merit to pray with <span class="key-term">kavanah</span> and to connect his prayer to the <span class="key-term">Tzadikay emes</span> who are the aspect of the great Lender. For this is the essential discharge of the loans of every person — for all people in the world are in the aspect of borrowers from the great Lender, who borrow and receive from him all their influence and vitality, as above. And praiseworthy is the one who fulfills his obligation to discharge his own debts through the proper rectification of <span class="key-term">t'filah</span>, as above.</p>
<p>And how can one insert himself to guarantee on behalf of his fellow, who has come to a state of borrowing in the physical realm — which is drawn from having greatly blemished the service of <span class="key-term">t'filah</span>? And how can he guarantee on his behalf to discharge the loan? — since the essential discharge as it should be is through the rectification of <span class="key-term">t'filah</span>, whereby one connects his prayer properly to the great Lender, until all the powers and vitality return to their root, to the Word of Hashem — for there is the aspect of the discharge of all the loans of all who come into the world. And this is incumbent upon each person himself. For in proportion to the abundance and vitality that he receives — which is the aspect of a loan from the great Lender — so must he strengthen himself in his service until he returns and elevates the power to its root, which is the essential discharge of the loan in the spiritual realm, and through this the loan in the physical realm is also rectified.</p>
<p>And this is an obligation upon every person himself. But for another person it is very difficult to discharge the debts and loans of his fellow. For each one has a particular path and channel through which he receives his influence and vitality — which is the aspect of a loan — and through his channel he must elevate all the powers and vitality to their root through the rectification of <span class="key-term">t'filah</span>, which is the aspect of the discharge of the loan. And therefore it is difficult for another to discharge on behalf of his fellow — for how can he ascend through the channel of his fellow to discharge his debts?</p>

<div class="section-header">§24</div>
<p>And this is the aspect of the law explained in the Shulchan Aruch: A guarantor at the time of the giving of the money becomes obligated [without any additional act of acquisition]. [A guarantor] not at the time of the giving of the money does not become obligated except through a <span class="key-term">kinyan</span> [formal act of acquisition]. And its source is in the Mishnah at the end of <span class="key-term">Bava Basra</span> <span class="source">(175b)</span>, where Shimon ben Nanas said: "Behold, one who seizes his fellow by the throat in the marketplace" etc. — "for it was not on his trust that he lent him." And the <span class="key-term">Tanna</span> praised his words greatly and said: "One who wishes to become wise" etc. — "let him attend to Shimon ben Nanas."</p>

<div class="section-header">§25</div>
<p>And this is because the essential root of the <span class="key-term">arvus</span> [guarantorship/mutual responsibility] is drawn from the aspect of the receiving of the Torah, when all of Israel became guarantors for one another. And this is because at the root of the souls of Israel, they are all considered as one — as our Sages, <em>zichronam livrachah</em>, said on the verse: <span class="scripture">"All the souls that came to the house of Yaakov"</span> <span class="source">(Genesis 46:26)</span> etc. — that they are called one soul, for they are drawn from the source of unity. And therefore all of Israel are guarantors for one another in the receiving of the Torah.</p>
<p>But the rectification of this <span class="key-term">arvus</span> — that is, to discharge the debt that each one owes — its essential rectification is through the aspect of the aforementioned great Lender, whose prayer is in perfection, in the aspect of the radiance of <span class="key-term">Mashiach</span>, who is the Lender to each one. And he is the guarantor on behalf of each one — that ultimately they will discharge their loan-debts in perfection. For the Children of Israel who became guarantors for one another — most of them, virtually all, are unable to discharge the obligation of the <span class="key-term">arvus</span>, for it is enough if one can extricate oneself.</p>
<p>And therefore the essential thing is the <span class="key-term">Tzadikay emes</span> who are in the aspect of the aforementioned great Lender, who grasps the <span class="key-term">Keesay HaKavod</span> — which is the root of the souls of all Israel — and he is able to make <span class="key-term">Rosh HaShanah</span>, to judge each one favorably according to his place, because he is in the aspect of <span class="key-term">M'komo shel Olam</span> etc., as above. And he is the Lender and he himself is the guarantor on behalf of each one — that he will certainly discharge his loan. For this <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span>, the great Lender, is constantly good to all and never ceases his goodness and kindness. And even one who borrows from him and does not discharge [the loan] — he lends him yet more and more, numerous times without number — until ultimately he will open his hand and discharge everything to him.</p>
<p>And if, <span class="key-term">chas v'shalom</span>, even so one rebels against him and does not wish to discharge — the great Lender has the power to collect his debts from him forever, and he can find him, and he will be unable to hide from him. And even with a king of flesh and blood there is a paradigm of this in the physical realm — for example, if there is found a mighty king who is pious and exceedingly good, who bestows kindness without measure, and whose wealth and possessions and dominion are very, very great and broad, and whose way is to bestow kindness by lending to all who come and ask to receive his goodness. And as soon as they repay him, he lends them yet again in doubled measure. And even one who does not repay properly — immediately when he comes and asks him for another loan, he lends him again, so that he should open his hand. And so forever. But one who rebels against him and does not submit himself beneath him — he can find him wherever he is and collect his debts from whatever he has. And if it does not suffice, he punishes and chastises him according to the debts owed.</p>
<p>However, a king of flesh and blood can only punish him until his soul departs. And therefore sometimes he cannot collect from him properly according to his debts. And sometimes [the debtor] flees to a place where he cannot be found. But regarding Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span>, it is written: <span class="scripture">"There is no darkness and no shadow of death where the workers of iniquity may hide"</span> <span class="source">(Job 34:22)</span>. And therefore it is said regarding Him, <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span>: <span class="scripture">"Who is like Him, a Teacher?"</span> <span class="source">(Job 36:22)</span> — and as Rashi explained there: "a king of flesh and blood" etc. See there.</p>
<p>And the <span class="key-term">Tzadikay emes</span>, the aspect of the aforementioned great Lender, who merit the Word of Hashem — which is the root of everything — are included in Him, <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span>, and have an eternal dominion, which is the dominion of Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> — the aspect of <span class="scripture">"a Tzadik rules"</span> <span class="source">(II Samuel 23:3)</span>. And this <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span>, the great Lender, constantly lends to all who wish to receive his goodness — in the aspect of: <span class="scripture">"All day long he is gracious and lends"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 37:26)</span> etc. And even one who does not repay him properly — he lends him more, until he opens his hand and discharges [his debt]. And if he still does not discharge — he lends him yet more, in order to expand his hand. And so numerous times without measure — in the aspect of: <span class="scripture">"The wicked one borrows and does not repay, but the Tzadik is gracious and gives"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 37:21)</span> — that even though he does not repay, the <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span> is gracious and gives more and more until he opens his hand and repays. And so forever, for his goodness never ceases.</p>
<p>For this <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span> is in the aspect of <span class="key-term">M'komo shel Olam</span>, who judges everyone favorably according to his place. And therefore he always finds merit in him, to continue and bestow upon him great goodness and kindness from a very lofty and awesome place — in such a way that he will be able to open his hand to discharge his debts. And if even so the wicked borrower persists stubbornly and rebels against him, <span class="key-term">chas v'shalom</span>, and no longer wishes to submit beneath him to receive his goodness — he has the power and dominion to collect and exact payment from him forever, for the dominion of Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> is in his hand. As it is said: <span class="scripture">"There is no darkness… to hide there"</span>. And as it is written: <span class="scripture">"And if they dig down to the netherworld, from there My hand will take them"</span> <span class="source">(Amos 9:2)</span> etc.</p>
<p>But his way is to do good constantly. And he orchestrates causes and various cycles without measure, through the paths of his wondrous goodness and kindness — until [the person] will consent to submit himself and ask of him his goodness. And then he bestows upon him each time yet more kindness and goodness from an ever higher place — until ultimately everyone will return to Hashem and discharge their debts properly.</p>
<p>And therefore only <span class="key-term">Tzadikim</span> such as these, who are in the aspect of the great Lender, the aspect of the radiance of <span class="key-term">Mashiach</span> — they are able to be a guarantor on behalf of each and every one. For they have the power to help each one until he discharges to them etc., as above. And therefore the essential <span class="key-term">arvus</span> is drawn from the aspect of <span class="key-term">Mashiach</span>, who comes from Yehudah, who said: <span class="scripture">"I will be his guarantor"</span> <span class="source">(Genesis 43:9)</span> etc. For Yehudah is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Mashiach</span> — the essential guarantor on behalf of all Israel who are guarantors for one another. For he will rectify everything, as above.</p>

<div class="section-header">§26</div>
<p>And this is the aspect of: <span class="scripture">"What pledge shall I give you?"</span> And she said: <span class="scripture">"Your signet-ring, your cord, and your staff"</span> <span class="source">(Genesis 38:18)</span> etc. For they needed to draw the soul of <span class="key-term">Mashiach</span> into the world, as our Sages, <em>zichronam livrachah</em>, said. Therefore she mentioned a pledge — for he is the guarantor on behalf of all Israel, that they will discharge all their debts. For he will rectify everything, as above.</p>
<p>And the essential rectification is through <span class="key-term">t'filah</span> in perfection, as above — which one merits through the rectification of the three services explained there: <span class="key-term">emunah</span> [faith] (the opposite of <span class="key-term">avodah zarah</span>), the rectification of the <span class="key-term">bris</span> [covenant], and the rectification of <span class="key-term">sh'fichus damim</span> [bloodshed] (which is the aspect of "do not scorn any person" etc.), as is explained there.</p>
<p>And this is the aspect of: "Your signet-ring, your cord, and your staff." "Your signet-ring" — this is the aspect of the rectification of the seal, the sign of the holy <span class="key-term">bris</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">t'filin</span>, which are alluded to by "your signet-ring" — as our Sages, <em>zichronam livrachah</em>, said. For <span class="key-term">t'filin</span> are the aspect of <span class="scripture">"engravings of a signet — holy to Hashem"</span> <span class="source">(Exodus 28:36)</span> — which are made through the rectification of the seal of the <span class="key-term">bris</span>, as is brought elsewhere (in the Torah "Teekoo Emunah," see there). "Your cord" — this is the aspect of <span class="key-term">tzitzis</span>, the rectification of the garments, which are the aspect of <span class="key-term">kavod</span> [honor]. For Rabbi Yochanan called his garments <span class="aramaic">"m'chab'd'sai"</span> [those that honor me] <span class="source">(Shabbos 113a)</span> — which is the aspect of the rectification of "do not scorn any person," the aspect of: <span class="scripture">"Who is honored? He who honors the creations"</span> <span class="source">(Avos 4:1)</span> etc. "Your staff" — this is the aspect of <span class="key-term">emunah</span>. For the essential <span class="key-term">emunah</span> was revealed through the signs and wonders that Moshe performed with his staff, as it is written: <span class="scripture">"And this staff you shall take in your hand, with which you shall perform the signs"</span> <span class="source">(Exodus 4:17)</span> — through which the holy <span class="key-term">emunah</span> was revealed, as it is written: <span class="scripture">"And he performed the signs before the eyes of the people, and the people believed"</span> <span class="source">(Exodus 4:30–31)</span> etc. And therefore the holy Tribes are called <span class="key-term">matos</span> [staffs/tribes] — for they are the entirety of the Children of Israel who draw the holy <span class="key-term">emunah</span> into the world, which is the aspect of the staff of Moshe. Therefore "your staff" is the aspect of <span class="key-term">emunah</span>.</p>
<p>It emerges that these three — "your signet-ring, your cord, and your staff" — are the aspect of the rectification of the three aforementioned services, through which is the essential rectification of <span class="key-term">t'filah</span>, through which is the essential discharge of all debts. And this perfection will be completed through <span class="key-term">Mashiach</span>, who is the guarantor on behalf of all, to discharge everything, as above. And therefore she took the pledge — these three: "your signet-ring" etc. — as above.</p>

<div class="section-header">§27</div>
<p>And therefore a guarantor at the time of the giving of the money becomes obligated. For one who lends to his fellow and bestows kindness upon him — through this he is included in the aspect of the aforementioned great Lender, who is in the aspect of <span class="key-term">M'komo shel Olam</span>, where all the souls of Israel are all one. Because of this, all of Israel are guarantors for one another — and from there is the root of the <span class="key-term">arvus</span>, as above.</p>
<p>And therefore at the time of the giving of the money the guarantor becomes obligated. For the guarantor can only become obligated on behalf of his fellow through receiving from the root of the aforementioned <span class="key-term">arvus</span>. And therefore at the time of the giving of the money — when the lender gives money and bestows upon the borrower — then through this <span class="key-term">mitzvah</span>, the root of the kindness of the great Lender is aroused, which is drawn from <span class="key-term">M'komo shel Olam</span>, where everything is one, where is the root of the <span class="key-term">arvus</span>. And therefore specifically then can he insert a guarantor beneath him — because there everything is one, as above.</p>
<p>But after the giving of the money, he does not become obligated. For since the borrower has already borrowed and does not repay — which is drawn from his having become distant from his root — and he is unable to discharge his loans, as above — therefore, then it is impossible for the guarantor to become obligated. For how can one guarantee on behalf of his fellow, when he is in an entirely different place from him? One cannot guarantee on his behalf unless they ascend and connect to the roots of the souls of Israel — through which they are included in the aspect of <span class="key-term">M'komo shel Olam</span>, where everything is one, and where all the places and all the channels are included together. And therefore from there specifically one can draw a rectification to discharge even the debts of one's fellow — which is what the guarantor undertakes. And this is not merited except by the great Lender, as above.</p>
<p>And therefore at the time of the giving of the money, through the <span class="key-term">mitzvah</span> of the loan, one is included there in the aspect of the great Lender, as above. Therefore specifically then the guarantor is obligated without a <span class="key-term">kinyan</span>. But afterwards he cannot become obligated to guarantee on behalf of his fellow — for afterwards he becomes distant from there, as above.</p>

<div class="section-header">§28</div>
<p>And therefore after the giving of the money he does not become obligated except through a <span class="key-term">kinyan</span>. For all <span class="key-term">kinyanim</span> [acts of acquisition] are through garments, and formerly in Israel through a shoe, as it is written: <span class="scripture">"And this was formerly done in Israel regarding redemption… a man would remove his shoe"</span> <span class="source">(Ruth 4:7)</span> etc. For a <span class="key-term">kinyan</span> means that one will no longer change. And all changes are below, in the variation of the places — where falsehood and change have their grip. But above, in the aspect of the root of the souls of Israel where everything is one — there is no change or falsehood whatsoever, in the aspect of: <span class="scripture">"The remnant of Israel shall not do iniquity"</span> <span class="source">(Zephaniah 3:13)</span> etc.</p>
<p>And therefore they accept the <span class="key-term">kinyan sudar</span> [acquisition by cloth] — for the garments and clothing are in the aspect of the <span class="key-term">Keesay HaKavod</span>, the aspect of the root of the souls of Israel, as it is written: <span class="aramaic">"You have prepared garments for them, from which the souls fly forth to human beings"</span> etc. <span class="source">(Zohar)</span>. And therefore all <span class="key-term">kinyanim</span> are through garments — through which one connects oneself to the root of the souls of Israel, where there is no change or falsehood, <span class="key-term">chas v'shalom</span>. And therefore after the <span class="key-term">kinyan</span> one cannot change anymore. And one who changes afterwards is as though he uproots himself from the root of the souls of Israel, <span class="key-term">chas v'shalom</span>.</p>
<p>And therefore through a <span class="key-term">kinyan</span> the guarantor becomes obligated even after the giving of the money. For through the <span class="key-term">kinyan</span> they ascend to the root of the souls of Israel, where everything is one, where all the souls are included together, one within the other — through which each one is a guarantor on behalf of his fellow, for from there is the root of the <span class="key-term">arvus</span>, as above.</p>

<div class="section-header">§29</div>
<p>And this is alluded to in what Shimon ben Nanas said regarding this law — that a guarantor not at the time of the loan does not become obligated: "Behold, one who seizes his fellow by the throat in the marketplace" etc. And the matter is puzzling at first glance — why did he describe specifically the case where one's fellow demands payment and he does not pay by the term "one who seizes his fellow by the throat"? Is it not possible without this [vivid imagery]?</p>
<p>But all their words are lofty and profound mysteries. And whatever allusions one can find regarding the service of the Creator, <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span>, in their holy words — each person is obligated according to his level to strive and find whatever he can. And according to the above, it connects well.</p>
<p>For this borrower who does not repay — which is drawn from not having returned all the powers and the vitality and the abundance that he received from the great Lender to their root, as above — from this blemish it results that he borrows and does not repay, as above. Therefore he is considered as though he is being choked at his throat. For the neck and throat of a person — where all the vitality of a person depends — through there one receives all the loans, which are the vitality and the powers. And through there one must seek to elevate them to their root. For all the influences and the powers that each one receives are all for the sake of his vitality and sustenance, which depends upon his neck — through which he receives food into his body, which is his vitality and sustenance. And as it is written: <span class="scripture">"All the toil of a person is for his mouth"</span> <span class="source">(Ecclesiastes 6:7)</span> — through which he receives vitality for his entire body.</p>
<p>And through there he must return the vitality to its root — which is the aspect of the discharge of debts. The essential rectification is through <span class="key-term">t'filah</span> — which is the Word of Hashem that issues from his neck and throat, as it is written: <span class="scripture">"Call out with your throat, do not hold back!"</span> <span class="source">(Isaiah 58:1)</span>. And it is written: <span class="scripture">"The exaltation of God is in their throat"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 149:6)</span> etc. For through speaking the exaltation of God with his throat and neck, through this he returns and elevates all the vitality and powers that he received through there — which is the aspect of the discharge of debts, as above.</p>
<p>And one who blemishes this and through this is unable to discharge the debts — he is considered as one who is being choked at his throat. That is, he is unable to bring in the breath of life and to bring out the breath of life — which is the essential vitality of a person, as is known — in the aspect of: <span class="aramaic">"the breath ascends, the breath descends"</span>, as above.</p>
<p>And this borrower who blemishes this and cannot discharge is considered as literally choked at his throat, as above. And this is what the <span class="key-term">Tanna</span> said regarding the guarantor who [guaranteed] not at the time of the giving of the money — that he does not become obligated: "Behold, one who seizes his fellow by the throat" etc. For since he demands payment and he does not discharge — behold, he is in the aspect of actual choking. And because of this it is impossible to guarantee on his behalf anymore — since he is already far from his root, where everything is one, where is the root of the <span class="key-term">arvus</span>, as above. But now that he has already been cut off from his vitality — through not elevating the vitality to its root, which is the aspect of choking, as above — how is it possible to guarantee on his behalf? As above.</p>
<p>And therefore Achisofel, who rebelled against the kingdom of the House of David — which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Mashiach</span>, through whom is the rectification of the entire world to return and discharge everything to its root — and Achisofel who blemished this — therefore his punishment was by choking, as it is written: <span class="scripture">"And he set his house in order, and he was choked"</span> <span class="source">(II Samuel 17:23)</span>. For he severed the vitality from its root by having rebelled against David, who is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Mashiach</span> — which is the aspect of choking, as above.</p>

<div class="translator-summary">
<h3>Summary of Uraiv 5 — Part 1 (§§23–29)</h3>
<p>§23: Chazal: "harchaik min ha'airavonos." All loans drawn from blemish in t'filah (not praying with kavanah, not connecting to Tzadik = Malveh HaGadol). All worlds/galgalim/shemesh/l'vanah = borrowers from him. Siluk chov = through t'filah (connecting ko'chos to shoresh through Tzadik). Pgam → loans/debts. Hard to guarantee for another — each person has his own sh'vil/tzinor through which he receives hashpa'ah, and through his own tzinor must elevate ko'chos/chiyus to shoresh = siluk halva'ah. How can another ascend through another's tzinor?</p>
<p>§24: Erev bish'as matan ma'os mishtabed; shelo bish'as matan ma'os aino mishtabed ela b'kinyan. Source: Mishnah (end Bava Basra), Shimon ben Nanas: "haray hachonaik es chavayro bashuk...shakain lo al emunaso hilahu." Tanna praised: "harotzeh sheyachkim...y'shamesh es Shimon ben Nanas."</p>
<p>§25: Shoresh arvus = kabbalas haTorah (kol Yisroel areivim zeh lazeh). Because at shoresh nishmos Yisroel all are one ("kol hanefesh haba'ah l'vais Yaakov" = nefesh echad). Tikkun arvus = through Malveh HaGadol = hisnatzetzus Mashiach. He is Lender AND Guarantor — his goodness never ceases (lends more and more even when not repaid). Mashal: mighty king, chasid v'tov, go'mail chesed, wealth v'memshalah g'dolah. Lends to all → repay → lends more. Even who doesn't repay → still lends more. Who rebels → can find him anywhere and collect. But melech basar vadam = only punish until soul departs + debtor can flee. Hashem: "ain choshech v'ain tzalma'ves l'hisasair sham" (Iyov 34:22); "mi chamohu moreh" (Iyov 36:22, Rashi). Tzadikay emes = niklalim in Hashem = memshalah nitzchis = "Tzadik moshail." "Kol hayom chonaim umalveh" (T'hilim 37:26). "Loveh rasha v'lo y'shalaim v'Tzadik chonaim v'no'sain" (T'hilim 37:21). M'komo shel Olam → always finds z'chus → bestows more. Even if rebels → power to collect forever ("v'im yacht'ru bish'ol misham yadi tikachaim," Amos 9:2). But darko l'haitiv tamid → orchestrates sibos v'gilgulim until submits → ultimately all return. Therefore only Tzadikim b'chinas Malveh HaGadol = hisnatzetzus Mashiach can guarantee for all. Ikkar arvus = from Mashiach = Yehudah: "anochi e'er'venu" (B'reishis 43:9).</p>
<p>§26: "Mah ha'airavon asher etain lach...chosamcha ufs'ailecha umatecha" (B'reishis 38:18). They needed to draw nishmas Mashiach (Chazal). She mentioned airavon = he is guarantor for all. Tikkun through shlosh avodos: (1) "chosamcha" = tikkun chosam bris kodesh = t'filin = "pituchay chosam kodesh laShem" (Sh'mos 28:36, made through tikkun bris); (2) "ufs'ailecha" = tzitzis = tikkun l'vushim = kavod ("Rabbi Yochanan kara l'manay m'chab'd'sai," Shabbos 113a) = "al t'hi vaz" = "aizehu m'chubad ham'chabed es habrios" (Avos 4:1); (3) "umatecha" = emunah (staff of Moshe = osos umof'sim, "v'es hamateh hazeh tikach b'yadcha asher ta'aseh bo es ha'osos," Sh'mos 4:17 → "vaya'as ha'osos...vaya'amain ha'am," Sh'mos 4:30-31). Sh'vatim called "matos" = draw emunah. These 3 = tikkun shlosh avodos → t'filah → siluk chovos → completed through Mashiach = guarantor. Therefore she took these 3 as pledge.</p>
<p>§27: Erev bish'as matan ma'os mishtabed: lending = g'milas chesed → niklal in Malveh HaGadol = M'komo shel Olam (kol nishmos Yisroel = echad = shoresh arvus). At matan ma'os → mitzvah arouses shoresh chesed from M'komo shel Olam → erev mishtabed without kinyan. After matan ma'os → borrower already far from shoresh → cannot guarantee (each in different makom) → only possible when ascending to shoresh nishmos where all one + all tzinoros together. Only Malveh HaGadol merits this. At matan ma'os → niklal there through mitzvah → erev obligated. Afterwards → distant → cannot.</p>
<p>§28: After matan ma'os → erev only through kinyan. Kinyanim = through b'gadim (formerly: min'al, "v'zos l'fanim b'Yisroel al hag'ulah...shalaf ish na'alo," Rus 4:7). Kinyan = will not change again. Shinuyim = l'matah, in shinui ham'komos (grip of sheker). L'maalah at shoresh nishmos Yisroel (all one) = no shinui/sheker ("sh'airis Yisroel lo ya'asu avlah," Tz'fanyah 3:13). Kinyan sudar: b'gadim = Keesay HaKavod = shoresh nishmos ("l'vushin t'kinas l'hon d'minaihu parchain nishmatin livnay nasha," Zohar). All kinyanim through b'gadim → connect to shoresh nishmos (no shinui). After kinyan → cannot change (would be uprooting from shoresh nishmos). Therefore through kinyan, erev obligated even after matan ma'os → ascend to shoresh nishmos = all one = arvus.</p>
<p>§29: Shimon ben Nanas: "haray hachonaik es chavayro bashuk" — why use chonaik [choking]? All their words = razin ila'in. The borrower who doesn't repay = neechnok big'rono: neck/throat = where all chiyus depends, through there receives halva'os (= chiyus/ko'chos), through there must elevate to shoresh. Food enters through mouth/throat ("kol amal ha'adam l'fihu," Koheles 6:7). Chiyus returns through t'filah = d'var Hashem from throat ("k'ra v'garon al tachsoch," Yeshayahu 58:1; "rommos Eil bigronam," T'hilim 149:6). Speaking rommos Eil → returns ko'chos to shoresh = siluk chovos. Pgam → unable to bring in/out ru'ach chayim = choking ("hevel salik hevel nachis"). Therefore Tanna: borrower who doesn't pay = chonaik = already far from shoresh (all one / arvus) → cannot guarantee. Achisofel rebelled against malchus Bais David (= Mashiach = tikkun ha'olam) → punishment = chenek ("vay'tzav el baiso vayaicha'naik," II Shmuel 17:23) = severed chiyus from shoresh.</p>
<p class="translator-note">Based on Likutay Moharan II:1 "Teekoo" · Hilchos Uraiv 5 — Part 1 (§§23–29). Part 2 continues with §§30–36.</p>
